<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="The story of Gunnar Asmussen: Olympic champion and machinist, then Aarhus bicycle dealer and the yellow-and-black Super Prestige funny bike."/><meta property="og:image" content="https://teob.dk/images/bikes/asmussen-super-prestige/hero.webp"/><title>When Gunnar Asmussen made the bicycle lower · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com"/><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin/><link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Sans:wght@400;500;600&family=Libre+Caslon+Display&display=swap" rel="stylesheet"/><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head><body><EnglishHeader current="stories" danishPath="/historier/gunnar-asmussen-funny-bike/"/><main><article class="skibby-page">
<header class="skibby-hero"><p class="section-label">Danish bicycle craft · 1968–1988</p><h1>When Gunnar Asmussen<br/>made the bicycle <em>lower</em></h1><p>An Olympic champion, a workshop in Aarhus and a bicycle from the years when aerodynamics was allowed to change the shape itself.</p></header>
<figure class="skibby-image"><img src={`${base}/images/bikes/asmussen-super-prestige/hero.webp`} alt="Yellow and black Asmussen Super Prestige time-trial bicycle with a small front wheel and large rear wheel"/><figcaption>Asmussen Super Prestige, about 1988. The bicycle is in the collection.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="skibby-opening"><p class="section-label">Wrong on purpose</p><p>The small front wheel, sloping top tube and low front make the bicycle look uneven. That is precisely the point.</p></section>
<section class="skibby-story"><aside><strong>1968</strong><span>From the workshop<br/>to Mexico City</span></aside><div>
<h2>A machinist on Denmark’s gold-medal team</h2><p>Gunnar Henry Asmussen trained as a machinist at Sabroe in Aarhus while becoming one of Denmark’s strongest track riders. At the 1968 Mexico City Olympics, he won gold with the Danish 4,000-metre team pursuit squad. It was a discipline in which position, pace and precision had to work like parts of a mechanism.</p><p>It is tempting to draw a straight line from the machinist to the bicycles that later carried his name. The documentation is not that simple. But his practical eye for metal, measurements and function followed him into the next part of his life.</p>
<h2>A bicycle name in Aarhus</h2><p>Asmussen opened a bicycle shop in Aarhus in the late 1970s. From 1981 it stood at Fredensgade 54. It sold more than established brands. Bicycles also appeared with the Asmussen name on the down tube, often in black, yellow or strong colours.</p><p>They occupied a space between international racing bicycle and local craft. Components might be Italian, but the name, fitting and meeting with the rider belonged in Aarhus.</p><blockquote>The funny bike was not strange for strangeness’s sake. It was an attempt to make rider and machine occupy less space in the wind.</blockquote>
<h2>When front wheels became smaller</h2><p>During the 1980s, the time-trial bicycle became an open experiment. Francesco Moser’s hour records in 1984 brought large rear wheels, smaller front wheels and a low riding position to a wide audience. Wind tunnels, aero helmets and new wheel shapes shifted attention from bicycle weight to the air through which rider and machine had to travel.</p><p>A smaller front wheel allowed lower handlebars. The sloping top tube followed the low front while the large rear wheel retained a familiar rolling circumference. The result became known as a <em>funny bike</em>: an informal name for a serious pursuit of seconds.</p></div></section>
<section class="skibby-facts">{facts.map(([n,l])=><div><strong>{n}</strong><span>{l}</span></div>)}</section>
<section class="skibby-context"><div><p class="section-label">The bicycle in the collection</p><h2>A Danish answer to an international idea</h2></div><div><p>The surviving Asmussen Super Prestige is dated to about 1988. Its yellow-and-black steel frame uses funny-bike geometry and carries parts from Campagnolo’s Croce d’Aune and C-Record families. Cinelli bars and stem continue the Italian theme.</p><p>It is displayed with a period Brancale aero helmet. The helmet suits the same idea, but is not documented as the bicycle’s original companion.</p></div></section>
<figure class="skibby-image"><img src={`${base}/images/bikes/asmussen-super-prestige/bike-and-helmet.webp`} alt="Asmussen Super Prestige funny bike displayed with a white Brancale aero helmet"/><figcaption>The bicycle and Brancale aero helmet show how comprehensive the pursuit of lower drag became.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="skibby-return"><p class="section-label">What we do not know</p><blockquote>The Asmussen name is certain. The precise hands behind every joint on this frame are not documented.</blockquote><div><p>Gunnar Asmussen had bicycles made under his own name, but no workshop record or invoice has yet been found to identify who designed, cut and assembled this particular frame. It should therefore be described as an Asmussen model, not automatically as a frame he personally built from the ground up.</p><p>The uncertainty does not make the bicycle less interesting. It reminds us that Danish bicycle craft was also a network of shops, framebuilders, importers and mechanics.</p></div></section>
<section class="skibby-finish"><div><p class="section-label">After the experiment</p><h2>The shape was brief. The idea remained.</h2></div><div><p>Funny bikes enjoyed a relatively short heyday. Rules became tighter, and modern time-trial bicycles found other ways to make the rider low and narrow. But the basic idea survived: the bicycle could no longer be judged alone. Rider, helmet, wheels and frame formed one aerodynamic system.</p><p>The Asmussen Super Prestige therefore stands as something more specific than a curious Danish bicycle. It connects an Olympic champion and an Aarhus workshop with the international 1980s attempt to shape the entire bicycle around the wind.</p><p><a href={`${base}/en/periods/1980-1989/`}>Read about the rise of aerodynamics in the 1980s .</a></p></div></section>
